<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.2.2: Multiplying Binomials</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01584</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.2.2: Multiplying Binomials</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ed4397d9-3332-4d2e-b0d3-d01948755602</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (20 minutes)</title>
<para>This activity introduces three different ways to multiply binomials together. Students should familiarize themselves with each method. Then they can decide which method works best for them and use the others to check their multiplication.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>
  <emphasis>Digital Modeling Strategy</emphasis> - To model the use of algebra tiles, have students access the algebra tiles in Polypad at <link url="https://polypad.amplify.com/p">https://polypad.amplify.com/</link>. Introduce the different types of tiles that students can access from the left side navigation bar. 
  </para><item>The unit tile is the small yellow tile that has dimensions of 1 unit by 1 unit.</item> 
  <item>The linear tile, or \(“x”\) tile, has dimensions of 1 unit by \(x\) units.</item>
  <item>The quadratic tile, or \(“x^2”\) tile, has dimensions of \(x\) units by \(x\) units.</item>
  <item>Each of the tiles also have an additive inverse tile: \(-1\), \(-x\), and \(-x^2\).</item>

<para>  As with the other methods, go step by step through the process using the algebra tiles. Begin by asking students to drag the algebra tiles needed for the first binomial into the work area. Circulate around the room to check which tiles students have selected. Watch that students have correctly pulled the units representing - 7. Next, have students line the tiles up with the variable tiles first and the unit tiles at the end. Repeat the process for assembling the terms for the second binomial.</para>

<image src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ce74950a3d545cc5d494613b120f301e57b79213" width="750"/><para>Be sure students line up one binomial as the length of a rectangle and the other as the width of the rectangle. The area of the rectangle they define represents the product of the binomials. Ask students to identify the terms one at a time and then drag the representation of the product into the area model. This process should appear similar to students since it mimics the process they used in the Unit's Inquiry Project. Use questions to scaffold the process such as the ones below. </para>

<list>
  <item>What is the result of \(x \cdot x\)? What tile represents \(x^2\)?</item>
  <item>What is the result of \(1 \cdot x\)? \(-1 \cdot 1\)? \(-1 \cdot x\)? etc. Which tile represents this product?</item> 
</list>
<para>Note that holding the “shift” key while selecting multiple tiles will create a group. A pop-up menu allows you to make a copy of what you have selected so that you can fill in terms more quickly. The pop-up menu also gives you the chance to negate a value if the wrong tile was selected. </para>

<image src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7b3650b98ce8316986060be75c61bfa1dbe4fef5" width="400"/><para>Students may have difficulty understanding why some of the terms have a negative value and why some are positive. Stress that this is an area model so the product is the result of the parts they identified along the length and width of the rectangle. </para>

<para>Once the rectangle is complete, It is sometimes helpful to move the factors away from each side of the rectangle so students can focus on the product (resulting rectangle). </para>

<image src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/5a3f6f7e44d5ffd38ff8d9e63f3a355e3a5fcba9" width="400"/><para>Be sure to have students determine the simplified final product. It may be helpful to have students write out each of the similar terms separately, if a scaffold is needed. In this example, students may write \(2x^2 + 4x + -7x + -7x + 4(-7)\) before arriving at the final answer of \(2x^2 -10x - 28\).</para>


<para>As a class, examine the three methods used in the table to multiply \((x-7)(2x+4)\). Go step by step through each method while asking for questions after each method. Then allow students to work through the exercises individually.</para>
<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para><title>Support for English Language Learners</title></para>
    <para><title/><!--Extra Support Name-->MLR 3: Clarify, Critique, Correct: Conversing</para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body"> 
    <!--Support Content-->
    <para>After discussing the three methods to multiply \((x-7)(2x+4)\)  as a class, present an incorrect answer and explanation. For example, " \((x+8)(2x+3)= x^2+24\) because when you multiply two binomials, you multiply the first terms and the last terms.” Ask students to identify the error, critique the reasoning, and write a correct explanation. Invite students to share their critiques and corrected explanations with the class. Listen for and amplify the language students use to explain how to find the the difference of squares. This helps students evaluate, and improve upon, the written mathematical arguments of others.</para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize"><!--Support Italics-->Design Principle(s): Optimize output (for explanation); Maximize meta-awareness</para>
    <para class="os-raise-extrasupport-title"><!--Extra Support Title-->Learn more about this routine</para>
<!-- Extra Support Content--><para><link url="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lozZJ21i3mQ;&amp;rel=0">View the instructional video</link> and <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/36e324584ae01e4eafc6674ac4ec02b4ae99002c">follow along with the materials </link>to assist you with learning this routine.</para>
       <para class="os-raise-extrasupport-title"><!--Extra Support Title-->Provide support for students</para>
    <para><link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/76d1756428038b737f48dc515486cddceb99e2f8">Distribute graphic organizers</link> to the students to assist them with participating in this routine.</para>
  </div>
</div>
<title> Student Activity</title>
<para>The binomials in the expression \((x-7)(2x+4)\) are solved three different ways in the table. Describe how the two binomials are being multiplied. Multiply each binomial. Use the "^" symbol to enter an exponent.</para>
<para>Students are also provided with this video if they want help with the initial example</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <iframe><video controls="true" crossorigin="anonymous">
      <source src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f7497b4b5abbe1e66c6824a9d34edeed4d604ee4">
      <track default="true" kind="captions" label="On" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/51d0d48cbf3748fea9b3f4ced85739724b6e9f84" srclang="en_us">
    </track></source></video></iframe>
</div>
<table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Solution</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Description of Solution.</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\((x-7)(2x+4)\)</para>
        <para>\(x(2x+4)-7(2x+4)\)</para>
        <para>\(2x^2+4x-14x-28\)</para>
        <para>\(2x^2-10x-28\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para><emphasis>First,</emphasis> ______________________</para>
        <para><emphasis>Second,</emphasis> ___________________</para>
        <para><emphasis>Next,</emphasis> ___________________</para>
        <para>So, the solution is \(2x^2-10x-28\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para> \(\begin{align*}
          x &amp;- 7 \\
          \underline{\times \;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;2x}&amp;\underline{~+ 4\;\;\;} \\
          4x &amp;- 28\\
          \underline{2x^2 -14x} &amp;  \underline{\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;}\\
          2x^2 - 10x &amp;-28 \\
          \end{align*}\) </para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para><emphasis>First,</emphasis> ______________________</para>
        <para><emphasis>Second,</emphasis> ___________________</para>
        <para><emphasis>Third,</emphasis> ____________________</para>
        <para><emphasis>Next,</emphasis> ___________________</para>
        <para>So, the solution is \(2x^2-10x-28\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <image alt="IMAGE SHOWING MULTIPLICATION OF THE BINOMIALS, WITH AN ARROW GOING FROM X TO 2X, AN ARROW GOING FROM X TO 4, AN ARROW GOING FROM NEGATIVE 7 TO 2X, AND AN ARROW GOING FROM NEGATIVE 7 TO 4." class="img-fluid atto_image_button_text-bottom" height="48" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/17c1f054ecdff8655d1c4473c0338a841a486bcc" width="91"/><para>\(2x^2+4x-14x-28\)</para>
        <para>\(2x^2-10x-28\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para><emphasis>First,</emphasis> ______________________</para>
        <para><emphasis>Second,</emphasis> ___________________</para>
        <para><emphasis>Third,</emphasis> ____________________</para>
        <para><emphasis>Next,</emphasis> ___________________</para>
        <para>Combine like terms, and the solution is \(2x^2-10x-28\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<para>Write down your answers, then discuss your steps with a partner. Do you agree on all of the steps? If not, make changes where needed.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Solution</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Description of Solution.</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\((x-7)(2x+4)\)</para>
        <para>\(x(2x+4)-7(2x+4)\)</para>
        <para>\(2x^2+4x-14x-28\)</para>
        <para>\(2x^2-10x-28\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para><emphasis>First</emphasis>, the second binomial \((2x + 4)\) is distributed to both of the terms in the first binomial.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Second</emphasis>, individual terms are distributed.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Next</emphasis>, the like terms \(4x\) and \( –14x\) are combined.</para>
        <para>So, the solution is \(2x^2-10x-28\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para> \(\begin{align*}
          x &amp;- 7 \\
          \underline{\times \;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;2x}&amp;\underline{~+ 4\;\;\;} \\
          4x &amp;- 28\\
          \underline{2x^2 -14x} &amp;  \underline{\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;}\\
          2x^2 - 10x &amp;-28 \\
          \end{align*}\) </para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para><emphasis>First</emphasis>, the binomials are written vertically.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Second</emphasis>, the last term of the bottom binomial is multiplied, or distributed, to each of the terms in the top binomial.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Third</emphasis>, the first term of the bottom binomial is multiplied, or distributed, to each of the terms in the top binomial, being sure to line up the like terms vertically.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Next</emphasis>, add the like terms. They should be “stacked” on top of each other.</para>
        <para>So, the solution is \(2x^2-10x-28\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <image alt="IMAGE SHOWING MULTIPLICATION OF THE BINOMIALS, WITH AN ARROW GOING FROM X TO 2X, AN ARROW GOING FROM X TO 4, AN ARROW GOING FROM NEGATIVE 7 TO 2X, AND AN ARROW GOING FROM NEGATIVE 7 TO 4." height="48" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/17c1f054ecdff8655d1c4473c0338a841a486bcc" width="91"/><para>\(2x^2+4x-14x-28\)</para>
        <para>\(2x^2-10x-28\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para><emphasis>First</emphasis>, distribute the first term of the front binomial to the first term of the back binomial (F).</para>
        <para><emphasis>Second</emphasis>, distribute the first term of the front binomial to the second term of the back binomial (O).</para>
        <para><emphasis>Third</emphasis>, distribute the second term of the front binomial to the first term of the back binomial (I).</para>
        <para><emphasis>Next</emphasis>, distribute the second term of the front binomial to the second term of the back binomial (L).</para>
        <para>Combine like terms, and the solution is \(2x^2-10x-28\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<para>Multiply each binomial. Use the "^" symbol to enter an exponent.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((a+6)(a-4)\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(a^2+2a-24\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((5b+9)(4b+3)\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(20b^2+51b+27\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((5x^2-1)(2x-8)\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(10x^3-40x^2-2x+8\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> \((7mn+1)(11mn-3)\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(77m^2n^2-10mn-3\)</para>
<title>Student Facing Extension</title>
<title>Are you ready for more?</title>
<para>Multiply the binomials. Add like terms to simplify the expression.</para>
<para>\((5x^2y-xy)(3x+y)+(6x+4y)(-3x^2y+2xy)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para>\(\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;=15x^3y+5x^2y^2-3x^2y-xy^2-18x^3y+12x^2y-12x^2y^2+8xy^2\) </para>
<para>\(\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;=-3x^3y+9x^2y-7x^2y^2+7xy^2\)</para>
<title>Anticipated Misconceptions</title>
<para>When students use the Distributive Property, emphasize that the sign combining the new products is determined by the sign in the binomial that is broken up. The example from the table is shown below, with the corresponding signs in red:</para>
<para>\((x\;{\style{color:red}-}\;7)(2x+4) = x(2x+4)\;{\style{color:red}-}\;7(2x+4)\)</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Ask students to explain which method they prefer. As students discuss each method, emphasize the following points:</para>
<list>
  <item> To use the Distributive Property, the first binomial is broken up so that each term is multiplied by the second binomial. </item>
  <item> When using Vertical Alignment, it is important to align like terms as you write the individual products. This will help you combine them into the correct final product. </item>
  <item> When using the FOIL method, you can view the process as 4 individual steps that are then combined to find the product. </item>
</list>
<title>6.2.2: Self Check</title>
<!--BEGIN SELF CHECK INTRO BEFORE Tables -->
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<!--SELF CHECK QUESTION GOES BEFORE THE Table -->
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para><!--QUESTION GOES HERE FROM TABLE --></para>
<!--SELF CHECK table-->
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Answers</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Feedback</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para><emphasis>QUESTION</emphasis>:</para>
        <para>Multiply the binomials.</para>
        <para>\((5y+2)(6y-3)\)</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para><emphasis>Answers</emphasis></para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para><emphasis>Feedback</emphasis></para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(30y^2-3y-6\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>That’s correct! Check yourself: Confirm your solution by multiplying the binomials a different way. You should find the same solution.</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(27y-6\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Did you multiply the first two terms together to get \(30y^2\)? The answer is \(30y^2-3y-6\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(30y^2-27y-6\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Did you use the correct signs when multiplying in each step? The answer is \(30y^2-3y-6\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>\(30y^2+3y+6\)</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Did you use the correct signs when combining like terms? The answer is \(30y^2-3y-6\).</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<!--END SELF CHECK INTRO BEFORE Tables -->
<title>6.2.2: Additional Resources</title>
<para><emphasis><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<title>Multiplying Binomials Using the Distributive Property</title>
<para>Just like there are different ways to represent multiplication of numbers, there are several different ways to represent how to multiply a binomial times a binomial using the Distributive Property of Multiplication over Addition.</para>
<para>Distribute to multiply the binomials.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
<para> \((y+5)(y+8)\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Distribute the binomial \((y+8)\).<image alt="." class="atto_image_button_text-bottom" height="58" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7966e305c4d5e6c86da2d248d6553821e131e92a" width="95"/> </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Now, distribute the individual terms.</para>
<para>  \(y^2+8y+5y+40\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Combine like terms.</para>
<para>  \(y^2+13y+40\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
<para>\((4y^2+3)(2y^2-5)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Distribute the binomial \((2y^2 - 5)\).</para>
<para>  \(4y^2{\style{color:red}(}{\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}y}^{\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}−}{\style{color:red}5}{\style{color:red})}+ 3(2y^2−5) \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis> Distribute the individual terms.</para>
<para>  \(8y^2-20y^2+6y^2-15\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Combine like terms.</para>
<para>  \(8y^4-14y^2-15\)</para>
<title>Multiplying Binomials Using the FOIL Method</title>
<para>If you multiply binomials often enough, you may notice a pattern. Notice that the first term in the result is the product of the <emphasis>first </emphasis>terms in each binomial. The second and third terms are the product of multiplying the two <emphasis>outer </emphasis>terms and then the two <emphasis>inner </emphasis>terms. And the last term results from multiplying the two <emphasis>last </emphasis>terms.</para>
<para>We abbreviate “First, Outer, Inner, Last” as FOIL. The letters stand for “First, Outer, Inner, Last.” We use this as another way to multiply binomials. The word FOIL is easy to remember and ensures we find all four products.</para>
<para>Let’s multiply \((x+3)(x+7)\) using both methods.</para>
<image height="283" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d7c2312fa4d131251848612a310e63d8fd07fe5c" width="503"/><para>We summarize the steps of the FOIL method below. The FOIL method only applies to multiplying binomials, not other polynomials!</para>
<note>
  <para> <emphasis> How to use the FOIL method to multiply two binomials:</emphasis></para>
  <hr/>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Multiply the <emphasis>First </emphasis>terms.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Multiply the <emphasis>Outer </emphasis>terms.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Multiply the <emphasis>Inner </emphasis>terms.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Multiply the <emphasis>Last </emphasis>terms.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 5 - </emphasis>Combine like terms, when possible.</para>
  <image alt="" class="img-fluid atto_image_button_text-bottom" height="110" role="presentation" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1a83b5709b636991315587e3b291cae421d5b554" width="300"/></note>
<para>When you multiply by the FOIL method, drawing the arrows will help your brain focus on the pattern and make it easier to apply.</para>
<para>Now we will review examples where we use the FOIL pattern to multiply two binomials.</para>
<para>Multiply the binomials using the FOIL method.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example 3</emphasis></para>
<para> \((y-7)(y+4)\)</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Multiply the \({\style{color:red}F}{\style{color:red}i}{\style{color:red}r}{\style{color:red}s}{\style{color:red}t}\) terms.</para>
<image alt="." class="img-fluid atto_image_button_text-bottom" height="83" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f1a02cae3957055064676c2bd8da34d72d00c602" width="300"/><para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Multiply the \({\style{color:red}O}{\style{color:red}u}{\style{color:red}t}{\style{color:red}e}{\style{color:red}r}\) terms.</para>
<image alt="." class="img-fluid atto_image_button_text-bottom" height="83" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/aaacae1260c37a89bc93957b0252ed0cbc316134" width="300"/><para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Multiply the \({\style{color:red}I}{\style{color:red}n}{\style{color:red}n}{\style{color:red}e}{\style{color:red}r}\) terms.</para>
<image alt="." class="img-fluid atto_image_button_text-bottom" height="83" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/25e5b5e545503ef27571d76458158cbf10bdfc44" width="300"/><para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Multiply the \({\style{color:red}L}{\style{color:red}a}{\style{color:red}s}{\style{color:red}t}\) terms.</para>
<image alt="." class="img-fluid atto_image_button_text-bottom" height="83" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/0da8f8d51fe9c77966a1fe5c5e869c97fa2978f1" width="300"/><para><emphasis>Step 5 - </emphasis>Combine like terms.</para>
<para>  \(y^2 - 3y - 28\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Example 4</emphasis></para>
<para> \((4x+3)(2x-5)\)</para>

<image alt="." class="img-fluid atto_image_button_text-bottom" height="44" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/644d37bb8f11684c2e248d051d713f2b41d7d205" width="100"/><para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Multiply the <emphasis>First </emphasis>terms, \(
  {\style{color:red}4}
  {\style{color:red}x} \;
  {\style{color:red}\cdot} \; {\style{color:red}2}
  {\style{color:red}x}
  \).</para>
<image alt="." class="img-fluid atto_image_button_text-bottom" height="65" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/066b093155abf00564a7e9db2521f62241791781" width="150"/><para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Multiply the <emphasis>Outer </emphasis>terms, \(
  {\style{color:red}4}
  {\style{color:red}x}\;
  {\style{color:red}\cdot}\;
  {\style{color:red}(}
  {\style{color:red}{-5}}
  {\style{color:red})}
  \).</para>
<image alt="." class="img-fluid atto_image_button_text-bottom" height="65" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/424ddbd13d16b4bfb8691ef93e4f40410f6034f2" width="150"/><para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Multiply the <emphasis>Inner </emphasis>terms, \(
  {\style{color:red}3}\;
  {\style{color:red}\cdot}\;
  {\style{color:red}2}
  {\style{color:red}x}
  \).</para>
<image alt="." class="img-fluid atto_image_button_text-bottom" height="60" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2bdab8ce4648f492713f35578594b9f5823dd6b2" width="150"/><para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Multiply the <emphasis>Last </emphasis>terms, \(
  {\style{color:red}3}\;
  {\style{color:red}\cdot}\;
  {\style{color:red}(}
  {\style{color:red}{-5}}
  {\style{color:red})}
  \).</para>
<image alt="." class="img-fluid atto_image_button_text-bottom" height="60" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f98afe9f4b05fd15f234c28fa71f4e251fc423c1" width="150"/><para><emphasis>Step 5 - </emphasis>Combine like terms.</para>
<para>The FOIL method is usually the quickest method for multiplying two binomials, but it <emphasis>only</emphasis> works for binomials. You can use the Distributive Property to find the product of any two polynomials.</para>
<title>Multiplying Binomials Using Vertical Alignment</title>
<para>Another representation that works for all polynomials is Vertical Alignment. It is much like the method you use to multiply whole numbers. Look carefully at this example of multiplying two-digit numbers.</para> <para>\(\underline{\underline{\;\;\;\;23\\\times46}\\\;\;138\;\;\;\mathrm{partial}\;\mathrm{product}\\\;\;920\;\;\;\mathrm{partial}\;\mathrm{product}}\\1058\;\;\;\mathrm{product}\)</para>
  <para>Start by multiplying 23 by 6 to get 138.</para>
  <para>    Finally, add the partial products.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example 5</emphasis></para>
<para> Multiply using Vertical Alignment: \((3y-1)(2y-6)\). It does not matter which binomial goes on the top. </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Multiply \(3y-1\) by \(-6\). </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Multiply \(3y-1\) by \(2y\). </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Add like terms. </para>
<para>Notice the partial products are the same as the terms in the FOIL method.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{l}\begin{array}{r}\;\;\left(3y-1\right)\\\underline{\times\left(2y-6\right)}\\{\style{color:red}-}\;{\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}8}{\style{color:red}y}\;{\style{color:red}+}\;{\style{color:red}6}\\{\style{color:blue}6}{\style{color:blue}y}^{\style{color:blue}2}\;{\style{color:blue}-}\;{\style{color:blue}2}{\style{color:blue}y}\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\\\hline6y^2-20y+6\end{array}\end{array}\)</para>
<para>We have now used three ways to multiply binomials. Be sure to practice each process, and try to decide which one you prefer.</para>
<title>Try It: Multiplying Binomials</title>
<para>In questions 1 - 2, multiply the binomials using any of the methods described.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((x+8)(x+9)\)
    <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Your answer many vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para><emphasis>Using the Distributive Property by distributing either a binomial or an individual term:</emphasis></para>
    <para>\((x+8)(x+9)\) </para>
    <para><emphasis>Using FOIL:</emphasis></para>
    <para>\((x+8)(x+9)\)</para>
    <para>\(x^2+9x+8x+72\)</para>
    <para>\(x^2+17x+72\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Using Vertical Alignment:</emphasis></para>
    <para>\(\underline{\underline{\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;x+8\\\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\times\;x+9}\\\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;9x+72\\ \;\;x^2+8x}\\x^2+17x+72\)</para>
  </item>
  <item> \((3c^2+4)(5c-2)\)
    <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Your answer many vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <emphasis>Using the Distributive Property by distributing either a binomial or an individual term:</emphasis>
    <para>\((3c^2+4)(5c-2)\) </para>
    <para><emphasis>Using FOIL:</emphasis></para>
    <para>\((3c^2+4)(5c-2)\)</para>
    <para>\(15c^3-6c^2+20c-8\)</para>
    <para>\(15c^3-6c^2+20c-8\)</para>
    <para><emphasis>Using Vertical Alignment:</emphasis></para>
    <para>\(\underline{\underline{\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;3c^2+4\\\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\times5c-2}\\\;\;\;\;\;\;-6c^2\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;-8\\15c^3\;\;\;\;\;\;\;+20c}\\15c^3-6c^2+20c-8\)</para>
  </item>
</list></section>
</content>
</document>
